<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apuani</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Apuani were a Ligurian people of the mountains of north-western Tuscany, between the Magra and Serchio valleys, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apuan-alps">Apuan Alps</a> keep their name. They resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> stubbornly in the early 2nd century BCE until, in 180 BCE, the consuls deported about 40,000 of them with their families to Samnium in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where they became the Ligures Baebiani and Corneliani.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Apuani are named almost only by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, who calls them Ligures Apuani or simply Apuani and records them from the campaign of 187 BCE. The name is formed on a pre-Roman place-name base, apua, which parallels names such as Genua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>. The material culture of the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a> and the Magra in the early Hellenistic age shows strong links with regions far to the north-west, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golasecca-culture">Golasecca culture</a>. The archaeologist Adriano Maggiani reads this as a sign that the Ligurians moved south into formerly Etruscan land around the 5th century BCE.</p><p>They held the hills above Pistoia, the Garfagnana, the upper Versilia and the Magra valley, and were closely linked with the Friniates on the Emilian slope of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>. At Ameglia, near the mouth of the Magra, the necropolis of Cafaggio has yielded some fifty-four tombs of the late 4th and 3rd centuries BCE, with spears, swords, helmets and gold jewellery of Etruscan inspiration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Second Punic War Rome turned against the Ligurians. In 187 BCE the consuls Gaius Flaminius and Marcus Aemilius Lepidus campaigned against the Apuani and Friniates, and in 186 BCE the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-marcius-philippus">Quintus Marcius Philippus</a> was ambushed in their country and lost some 4,000 men at a place afterwards called the Saltus Marcius. In 180 BCE the proconsuls Publius Cornelius Cethegus and Marcus Baebius Tamphilus received the surrender of about 12,000 Apuani and, with the Senate's leave, moved them to public land in Samnium. In 179 BCE the consul Quintus Fulvius Flaccus transferred a further 7,000.</p><p>The figures and the character of the transfer are debated. Mattia Balbo argues that the total is a Roman estimate reckoned as for a colony, while Alberto Barzanò saw a settlement the Ligurians accepted rather than a punishment. Resistance went on until 155 BCE, when the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-claudius-marcellus-consul-166-bce">Marcus Claudius Marcellus</a> held a triumph over the Apuani.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The deportees were settled on the ager Taurasinus, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a>. Whether they formed one community or two is disputed: John R. Patterson argued for a single city of the Ligures Baebiani et Corneliani. Surveys of the 1980s found land shared in lots of roughly equal size. The community survived into the Empire and set up its bronze Tabula alimentaria in 101.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Apuani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They lived as separate tribes, among them the Ingauni, Intimilii, <b>Apuani</b> and Statielli, in hill forts and upland villages, combining herding, small-scale farming and raiding.</blockquote><a href="/state/ligures">Ligures</a></li></ul></section>
